# CSS Animations

A transition animates a change from one value to another: a straight line from A to B. **CSS animations** give us much more control, because we can describe as many points along the way as we like.

## Keyframes

A CSS animation is defined by **keyframes**. A keyframe says what the element should look like at a particular point in the animation, given as a **percentage**: `0%` is the start and `100%` is the end.

Two keyframes behave much like a transition. Red at the start, blue at the end, and the browser works out everything in between:

```{html autorun}
<div class="two-frames"></div>
```
```+css
@keyframes redToBlue {
  0%   { background-color: red; }
  100% { background-color: blue; }
}
.two-frames {
  width: 200px; height: 60px;
  animation: redToBlue 3s linear infinite;
}
```

Add a keyframe in the middle and it becomes something a transition cannot do:

```{html autorun}
<div class="three-frames"></div>
```
```+css
@keyframes redYellowBlue {
  0%   { background-color: red; }
  50%  { background-color: yellow; }
  100% { background-color: blue; }
}
.three-frames {
  width: 200px; height: 60px;
  animation: redYellowBlue 3s linear infinite;
}
```

**Keyframes do not have to be evenly spaced.** Move the middle one to `10%` and the animation rushes from red to yellow and then crawls from yellow to blue:

```{html autorun}
<div class="uneven"></div>
```
```+css
@keyframes uneven {
  0%   { background-color: red; }
  10%  { background-color: yellow; }
  100% { background-color: blue; }
}
.uneven {
  width: 200px; height: 60px;
  animation: uneven 3s linear infinite;
}
```

And you can have as many as you like:

```{html autorun}
<div class="rainbow"></div>
```
```+css
@keyframes rainbow {
  0%   { background-color: red; }
  14%  { background-color: orange; }
  28%  { background-color: yellow; }
  42%  { background-color: green; }
  57%  { background-color: blue; }
  71%  { background-color: indigo; }
  85%  { background-color: violet; }
  100% { background-color: red; }
}
.rainbow {
  width: 200px; height: 60px;
  animation: rainbow 5s linear infinite;
}
```

### Several Properties at Once

A keyframe can set as many properties as you want, and all of them animate together:

```{html autorun}
<div class="grow"></div>
```
```+css
@keyframes grow {
  0%   { background-color: red;  width: 60px;  height: 60px; }
  100% { background-color: blue; width: 240px; height: 30px; }
}
.grow {
  animation: grow 3s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
}
```

### The Same Start and End, Different Journeys

Here is something worth noticing about keyframes. Two animations can begin and end identically and get there in completely different ways.

Both boxes below start as a wide rectangle and finish as a tall one. The first one changes its width and height; the second one rotates a quarter turn:

```{html autorun}
<div class="scaler"></div>
<div class="rotator"></div>
```
```+css
@keyframes byScaling {
  0%   { width: 160px; height: 40px; }
  100% { width: 40px;  height: 160px; }
}
@keyframes byRotating {
  0%   { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  100% { transform: rotate(90deg); }
}
.scaler, .rotator {
  width: 160px; height: 40px;
  background-color: #b00;
  margin: 30px 60px;
}
.scaler   { animation: byScaling 2s ease-in-out infinite alternate; }
.rotator  { animation: byRotating 2s ease-in-out infinite alternate; }
```

Same endpoints, very different motion. Describing the middle of an animation, not just its ends, is what keyframes are for.

(`transform` is a CSS property for moving, rotating, and scaling an element. `rotate(90deg)` is one of its functions; there are others such as `translateX` and `scale`.)

## Writing It Out

Defining an animation uses an **at-rule**, so called because it begins with `@`:

```css
@keyframes animationName {
  0%   { /* styles at the start */ }
  50%  { /* styles halfway */ }
  100% { /* styles at the end */ }
}
```

`0%` and `100%` can also be written **`from`** and **`to`**, which reads better when there are only two:

```css
@keyframes fade {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to   { opacity: 1; }
}
```

Defining an animation does not apply it to anything. To use it, set the **`animation`** property on a selector:

```text
animation: <name> <duration> <timing-function> <delay> <iteration-count> <direction> <fill-mode>
```

- **`name`** is the name from the `@keyframes` rule.
- **`duration`** in seconds, such as `2s`.
- **`timing-function`** is the same set as transitions: `ease` (the default), `linear`, `ease-in`, `ease-out`, `ease-in-out`, or a custom `cubic-bezier`.
- **`delay`** before it starts.
- **`iteration-count`** is how many times to run: `1` (the default), any number such as `4`, a fraction such as `1.5`, or **`infinite`**.
- **`direction`** is which way to play: `normal` (start to end), `reverse` (end to start), or `alternate` (forwards, then backwards, then forwards).
- **`fill-mode`** is what happens outside the animation's run: `forwards` keeps the last keyframe's styles after it ends, `backwards` applies the first keyframe's styles before it starts, and `both` does both.

`fill-mode` is the one that catches people out. Without it, an element **snaps back** to its original styles the moment the animation finishes:

```{html autorun}
<div class="no-fill">no fill-mode</div>
<div class="with-fill">fill-mode: forwards</div>
```
```+css
@keyframes slide {
  from { margin-left: 0; background-color: #b00; }
  to   { margin-left: 200px; background-color: #4a9; }
}
.no-fill, .with-fill {
  width: 160px; padding: 8px; color: white; margin-bottom: 8px;
  background-color: #b00;
}
.no-fill   { animation: slide 2s ease-in-out; }
.with-fill { animation: slide 2s ease-in-out forwards; }
```

Both slide across. The first jumps back to the start when it finishes; the second stays where it landed.

## Transitions or Animations?

Use a **transition** when a change is triggered by something (a hover, a class being added) and goes straight from one value to another. Use an **animation** when you need intermediate steps, when it should repeat, or when it should run without anything triggering it.

## Exercises

1. Write a `@keyframes` rule named `fade` that goes from an `opacity` of `0` to an `opacity` of `1`, then apply it to `.message` so it runs once over `1s` and **stays visible afterwards**.

    ```html id=page1
    <p class="message">Hello</p>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your keyframes here */

    .message {
      padding: 10px;
      background-color: #00274c;
      color: white;
      /* Apply your animation here */
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+solution
    @keyframes fade {
      from { opacity: 0; }
      to   { opacity: 1; }
    }

    .message {
      padding: 10px;
      background-color: #00274c;
      color: white;
      animation: fade 1s forwards;
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/@keyframes\s+fade/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Define a `@keyframes` rule named `fade`', 'Defined the keyframes');
    assertRule('.message').should('have.css', 'animation-name: fade').run('Apply the `fade` animation to `.message`', 'Applied the animation');
    assertRule('.message').should('have.css', 'animation-duration: 1s').run('The animation should last `1s`', 'Set the duration');
    assertRule('.message').should('have.css', 'animation-fill-mode: forwards').run('Add `forwards` so the element keeps the final keyframe\'s styles instead of snapping back', 'Kept the final styles');
    ```

2. Write a three-keyframe animation named `pulse` that takes `.dot` from an `opacity` of `1`, to `0.2` at the halfway point, and back to `1`. Apply it so it runs forever over `1.5s`.

    ```html id=page2
    <div class="dot"></div>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your keyframes here */

    .dot {
      width: 40px;
      height: 40px;
      border-radius: 20px;
      background-color: #b00;
      /* Apply your animation here */
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+solution
    @keyframes pulse {
      0%   { opacity: 1; }
      50%  { opacity: 0.2; }
      100% { opacity: 1; }
    }

    .dot {
      width: 40px;
      height: 40px;
      border-radius: 20px;
      background-color: #b00;
      animation: pulse 1.5s infinite;
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/@keyframes\s+pulse/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Define a `@keyframes` rule named `pulse`', 'Defined the keyframes');
    wrap(/50%/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Include a keyframe at `50%` for the dimmed state', 'Added a middle keyframe');
    assertRule('.dot').should('have.css', 'animation-name: pulse').run('Apply the `pulse` animation to `.dot`', 'Applied the animation');
    assertRule('.dot').should('have.css', 'animation-iteration-count: infinite').run('It should repeat forever, so use `infinite`', 'Set it to repeat forever');
    assertRule('.dot').should('have.css', 'animation-duration: 1.5s').run('Each cycle should take `1.5s`', 'Set the duration');
    ```

3. An animation moves an element across the screen, and the moment it finishes the element jumps back to where it started. Which property fixes that?

    ```{mcq}
    You want the element to stay where the animation left it. What do you set?
    correct: Correct! `animation-fill-mode: forwards` keeps the styles from the final keyframe after the animation ends. Without it, the element reverts to its normal styles as soon as the animation is over.
    wrong: Look back at the two sliding boxes and what distinguished them.
    [x] `animation-fill-mode: forwards`
    [ ] `animation-direction: normal`
    [ ] `animation-iteration-count: infinite`
    [ ] `animation-timing-function: ease-out`
    ```

4. When is a keyframe animation the right choice over a transition?

    ```{mcq}
    Which of these need a CSS animation rather than a transition? (Select all that apply)
    correct: Correct! Transitions go directly from one value to another in response to a change. Animations are for anything with intermediate steps, anything that repeats, and anything that should run without being triggered.
    wrong: Revisit "Transitions or Animations?".
    [x] A color that cycles through red, yellow, and blue in order
    [x] A loading spinner that repeats forever
    [x] Something that starts moving as soon as the page loads, with nothing triggering it
    [ ] A button that darkens when the pointer is over it
    ```

